hi there steve kaufman here um one  question i get asked all the time  is how do you get to speaking can you  talk about your experience in getting  from the sort of passive knowledge of  the language to where you're speaking uh  those of you who have been following my  channel here at youtube or who have read  my blog the linguist on language  will know that i  am a great believer in the value of  input based learning listening and  reading it's just so powerful so  inexpensive it's a great way to spend  your time in building up your language  competence  and building up your vocabulary building  up your ability to understand the  language  making the brain more familiar with the  language but  still we all want to speak the language  that's why we that's why we learn the  language we want to speak  and a lot of people have trouble  speaking or they think they have trouble  speaking  and so the question is how do you get to  speaking well the simple answer is  to speak well you have to speak a lot  okay and when you start speaking at  whatever level you start speaking you're  going to have difficulty  uh in my view uh if i put my most of my  emphasis on listening and reading and  accumulating vocabulary  that that's gonna move me along in my  speaking  more quickly than just relying on  speaking i need words if i want to speak  to people i want it to be a natural  conversation i want to understand what  they have to say  if i were i've been studying chuck now  for two and a half months i haven't  spoken to anyone in czech i haven't i  don't say anything if i were in the  czech republic i probably would speak to  some people but it would be very  difficult it wouldn't be a very  satisfying  experience because probably most many  people in the czech republic  speak  english better than i speak czech  and those that don't who only speak  czech they would come at me so fast in  check i wouldn't understand what they're  saying  so while i would do it  because i'm there and i'd want to take  advantage of the opportunity and  my victories would be few and far  between most of these  forays into speaking with the locals  would end in defeat i'd still do it  because it's good for you  uh but uh i see no need to do it i'm  here in canada i would rather wait until  i have built up my capability in the  language my comprehension skills  listening comprehension vocabulary and  then go and say six months from now and  then i will have a chance to have  meaningful conversations with people but  it doesn't matter some people don't have  the choice they have to start now  and even if you have spent a year  listening and reading when you first  start to speak it's difficult it's  difficult to summon up all these words  that you've been learning and phrases  and start using them but you at some  point you have to break the ice  and so it's very important when you do  start speaking  that you don't get discouraged uh in  everything we do  uh if we're playing a game of tennis  let's say and we play well or golf or or  anything  and we do well we're encouraged to do it  again if we don't do well  we kind of don't feel  very good about ourselves and we don't  want to go back to that experience  because it was a not so satisfactory  experience  uh but then with that attitude you'll  never improve so if i have a poor game  of tennis i feel a little bit badly but  at some level i've got to say i've got  to go and work harder to get better  the same with languages but what's more  with languages in tennis if you do  poorly your opponent defeated you maybe  whereas in languages you can take the  position that you always win  because every time you're using a  foreign language however in however  limited away  you're actually using a skill that you  have invested in and that you have  developed that you didn't have before  like if i speak in check and i don't  understand what the person is saying and  i can't say what i want to say and and i  know i'll forget the most obvious things  like how to say my name is and how are  you  uh but still the little bit that i did  get across or did understand  it's it's an achievement that's what  i've i've achieved over the last two and  a half months  uh plus i know that i understand a lot  when i'm doing my listening and reading  so i i tend not to feel badly about  myself not to say i'm no good or or  whatever i i just keep going at it  because i know  that if you continue to speak you will  improve part of the reason you continue  you will improve if you continue to  speak is because you are hearing you are  again listening which is so powerful  you're listening to what the native  speaker says  and you are noticing how the native  speaker says things that you were trying  to say and you will also notice your own  mistakes and that will make you more  attentive to those mistakes in your own  listening and reading so actually  speaking is a great way to sort of  leverage up your listening and reading  activities but make no mistake you're  going to go through a painful period  where some people will have no patience  to talk to you uh in the language that  you've been learning some will revert to  english some will get  unpleasant and some will be very nice  and patient and and you humor you for a  while  uh but it's going to be a bit of a tough  road uh the longer you spend listening  and reading and building up your  vocabulary the easier the transition is  going to be  to uh speaking but sometimes you don't  have the choice you've arrived in a  country you've got to start speaking  so uh  all i'm saying is there's no easy path  the way you improve your speaking is to  speak more now there are some other uh  things that can help obviously listening  and reading continued listening and  reading intensive listening and reading  when i lived in japan i started speaking  soon after i arrived there  to the extent that i could but the vast  majority of my learning time was still  spent listening and reading building up  my comprehension  skills in my vocabulary now the other  thing you can do is is  you know the words you're going to use  the active vocabulary is obviously a  limited set of your total passive  vocabulary you don't have to use all  your words you don't have to use all  your phrases find some handy words and  phrases that you like to build your  thoughts around you don't have to know  all the ways of saying evidently  obviously clearly  you know these these synonyms uh  which we tend to use in conversation  have one that you use obviously or in my  opinion it's my view just in my opinion  pick one that works for you so have a  number of these set phrases that you can  use  um  pronunciation some people worry about  pronunciation i think having good  phrasing is more important than  pronunciation but if you want to work on  your pronunciation i found  that if you can find some audio content  that you really like where you like the  voice you like the content you like the  intonation  uh  then it becomes almost like music and i  did this with chinese and i listened  over and over and over again to a very  limited range of content and i believe  that that really improved my tones in  chinese so you can do that kind of thing  there are people writing is very good  because when you write you have more  time to think so it gives you time to to  work out how to say things personally i  don't mind using google translate i know  there are a number of things that i  would like to say and check i don't know  how to say them i just type them in  english up comes google translate it's  80 right but it's good enough it gives  me a sense of how i would express these  things in the language and sometimes  i'll even save them into link and and  save those words and phrases for later  use uh there is this technique of  shadowing which i don't do  but where people listen and and repeat  what they're listening to  i just find it very difficult to  continue doing that i've done it on  occasion but  uh you know  the whole thing with language learning  boils down to where do you want to spend  your time  uh  if you have an unlimited amount of time  you can shadow read write listen speak  do everything but if you have an hour or  two a day you have to make a decision  and i find that i'm more motivated to  increase my vocabulary and increase my  comprehension skills and do more reading  rather than to walk around  you know shadowing or talking out loud i  tend not to do that  uh it's possible  that that speeds up the transition to  speaking i don't know because i don't do  it uh i find that  my approach of focusing on building up  my vocabulary  is good enough and then when i get the  opportunity to speak  i just go for it and stumble and make  mistakes and get beaten up and  and go right back at it again and  and it just gradually surprisingly you  you just start to improve  uh a big part of speaking well is to  notice uh how your language differs from  that of the native speaker so when  you're talking to someone you gotta  really  listen clearly to what they're saying  how they use words listen try to a lot  of people don't listen to the  pronunciation of the native language  they try and read it as if it were  english so if there's an a there it's  pronounced a  no  most languages a is pronounced ah so you  really have to pay attention  uh and then start using it try things  out  and not worry about it i think a big  part of of uh one other thing too is is  when you speak you know let go don't say  i'm this foreigner meek mild stumbling  uh  sounding foolish foreigner hey just i'm  here i'm speaking i'm communicating if i  can communicate fine if i can't  i'll do better next time like it's not  such a big deal now all of that is  easier said than done  but  someone asked me what do i do that's  that's what i do at some point i start  speaking and i don't worry about it but  even when i am increasing my speaking  activity i still put most emphasis on  listening and reading so there you have  it and if you have any questions or  comments please don't hesitate
 

